 nurses what if you don't have doctors  see what the pandemic has really done to  our health care heroes  come at a cost tonight at seven on  global  we continue our coverage of the  allegations of sexual misconduct in the  military  the parliamentary committee looking into  this has heard testimony from the woman  who is making the accusations  against former chief of the defense  staff general jonathan vance  we also learned that a key advisor to  justin trudeau was  made aware of the allegations our  mercedes stevenson is now putting  questions to the deputy prime minister  but before we get to what christopher  freeland had to say mercedes guide us  through the testimony  robin major kelly brennan shared  explosive testimony on thursday during  her appearance before a parliamentary  committee  brennan is the first woman who came  forward to global news  accusing the country's former top  military commander  of sexual misconduct brennan told the  committee that jonathan vance fathered  two children with her and did not pay  child support  she also said vance told her that as the  country's top soldier he was  untouchable and owned the military  police  global news spoke with vance in february  and at that time  he denied all allegations of sexual  misconduct  and he also denied having fathered  children with brennan deputy prime  minister christian freeland called  brennan's testimony deeply troubling  it's  clear to me as we've heard from numerous  reports in the past that there are some  deep problems in the canadian armed  forces  with sexual harassment and with  treatment of women  and that has to end we also learned this  week that the prime minister's closest  advisor his chief of staff katie telford  knew in 2018 that an allegation had been  made against  vance experts warned these kinds of  alleged secrets pose a major security  threat  leaving the country's top military  commander open to blackmail  defense minister harjud sajin has faced  intense criticism  for not doing more when he was made  aware of the concerns about vance's  behavior  but the deputy prime minister defended  both sajin and telford  along with others in her government who  knew of the concerns  these are very difficult  issues to handle especially when there  are  questions of confidentiality  and the due process  that everyone deserves and  while i had no knowledge of this  i have a lot of confidence in the people  who handled this difficult situation  the military will be creating a  top-level position to deal with sexual  misconduct and racism  but the government still hasn't  committed to an independent watchdog  that would report to parliament  a move seen as crucial by so many  experts and survivors of sexual  misconduct  robin mercedes stevenson in ottawa thank  you and you can watch the full interview  with deputy prime minister  christopherland  tomorrow on the west block bc has seen  more anti-asian hate crimes  per capita than anywhere on the  continent still ahead global digs into  the disturbing trend  you're watching global national  the origins of the new coronavirus in  china and disparaging comments made by  world leaders about the pandemic  have all inflamed racism against  canada's asian community  a federal report found over 1100  anti-asian hate incidents and those were  just the ones reported  not all are sophie louis has been  digging into the disturbing trend in a  special program called hidden hate  anti-asian racism sophie  robin the rise in anti-asian racism has  of course been seen across north america  but according to one report bc has had  more incidents per capita  than any other region in the continent  it can be things like graffiti  or hateful comments like kung flu or  china virus we heard that from former  president donald trump  a lot but as we know verbal assaults can  quickly escalate  and become physical what happens is that  you may have  a suspect or a person of interest who  today is just  angry and yelling at somebody and  perhaps  just giving us a glimpse of of some hate  related comments  and tomorrow that same person  decides i'm gonna i'm gonna do something  criminal i'm gonna assault the person  i'm gonna punch him in the head i'm  gonna  kick his car and that's pretty much what  happened to a woman named trixie ling  she was just  out for a walk in vancouver last may  when a man said racist and sexist  comments  to her and then spat in her face as she  walked by him  now almost a year later she hopes that  by speaking out and sharing her story  she can spark these more meaningful  conversations about racism  it really sparked something in me it's  almost like this catalyst  and particularly a lot of that was anger  like i had so much anger and rage that  somebody could do this to another person  like  being really the dignity of a person not  recognizing the humanity of another  person  and on our global news special on  anti-asian racism robin we'll hear from  more asian canadians  about their experiences governor general  adrian clarkson  will join us we'll also hear from nba  star jeremy lin  and others right across this country and  will explore the hidden hate that has  existed in canada  long before covet even began that's  tonight at 7 30.  robin all right good stuff we're looking  forward to it sophie  up next time flies when you're in  isolation we'll explain when global  national  continues  it's a full house at the international  space station where four astronauts just  arrived on the spacex crew dragon  capsule with two spacex crews on board  the station now has  11 astronauts it's the biggest crowd in  over a decade  but not for long on wednesday this first  spacex crew is set to fly back to earth  the newcomers will stay for six months  to help with scientific research  and upgrades to the station  a team of volunteers in france were  greeted with cheers at the end of their  40-day stay in a deep dark cave eight  men  and seven women took part in the  experiment they were completely isolated  with no light and no phones think about  it  no contact with the outside world no  concept  of time they had to follow their  biological clocks to know when to wake  up  when to sleep and when to eat for some  when the end came they thought there was  still a week or so left  scientists hope the study will reveal  more about how people adapt to drastic  changes in living conditions  and environments for me though  that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is barrier in bc  we'd love to see your corner of the  country so please  keep sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching good night  you
 

